Building GPT fdisk as native Android executables
================================================

The programs can be cross-compiled with the Android NDK into stand-alone
arm64 (or 32-bit ARM / x86_64) executables that run in Termux, an adb shell,
or any other Android userland.  They link against bionic's libc and statically
against libc++, so no extra runtime libraries need to be pushed to the device
besides the executables themselves.

Requirements
------------
* Android NDK r23 or later (tested with r29)
* make, a host C compiler, tar, xz, curl or wget
* About 11 MB of build dependencies plus source downloads

Quick build
-----------
From the top of the source tree:

    tools/build-android.sh

The script:

  1. Locates the NDK (--ndk, $ANDROID_NDK_HOME, $ANDROID_NDK_ROOT,
     ~/Android/Sdk/ndk/*, /opt/android-ndk*, /Application/android-ndk*).
  2. Downloads and cross-compiles popt 1.19 (sgdisk) and ncursesw 6.5
     (cgdisk) into android/deps/<abi>/.
  3. Builds gdisk, cgdisk, sgdisk, and fixparts with TARGET=android.
  4. Strips and copies them to dist/android-<abi>/.

Options:

    --abi arm64-v8a     target ABI (default; also armeabi-v7a, x86_64)
    --api 24            minimum Android API level (default 24, Android 7.0)
    --ndk /path/to/ndk  NDK location

Equivalent manual build (after the dependencies exist in android/deps):

    make TARGET=android NDK=/path/to/android-ndk

Install and run on a device
---------------------------
    adb push dist/android-arm64-v8a/gdisk \
             dist/android-arm64-v8a/sgdisk \
             dist/android-arm64-v8a/fixparts \
             /data/local/tmp/gptfdisk/
    adb shell chmod 755 /data/local/tmp/gptfdisk/{gdisk,sgdisk,fixparts}

Reading a partition table needs no special privileges:

    adb shell /data/local/tmp/gptfdisk/gdisk -l /data/local/tmp/disk.img

Writing a partition table on a block device requires root:

    adb shell su -c '/data/local/tmp/gptfdisk/sgdisk -p /dev/block/sda'

In Termux, copy the executables into $PREFIX/bin.  cgdisk needs a real
terminal (TERM=xterm-256color works); fallback terminfo entries for xterm,
screen, vt100, linux, and ansi are compiled into the ncurses build, so no
terminfo database is needed on the device.

Android-specific changes
------------------------
* android/include/uuid/uuid.h and android/uuid_compat.cc provide the
  libuuid functions gptfdisk needs (notably uuid_generate), since bionic
  ships no uuid/uuid.h.
* diskio-unix.cc now skips the BLKSSZGET ioctl for regular files.  Android
  returns EACCES rather than ENOTTY for that ioctl on disk-image files,
  which produced a warning on every sector-size query.  Disk images are
  treated as having 512-byte sectors.
* The Android build omits -luuid (replaced by the compat layer) and links
  popt/ncurses statically from android/deps/<abi>/.

Limitations
-----------
* The NDK's glob(3) is only available from API 28, so popt is built without
  glob support.  gptfdisk does not use popt configuration files, so this has
  no effect on sgdisk.
* Minimum API level is 24 unless you build with --api 21; some libc
  functions may then be unavailable.
